            <ArticleTitle>Cancer patients' understanding of longitudinal EORTC QLQ-C30 scores presented as bar charts.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2012-2017</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0738-3991(16)30336-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pec.2016.08.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate cancer patients' understanding of graphical presentations of longitudinal EORTC QLQ-C30 scores.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We conducted semi-structured interviews with brain tumour patients participating in routine patient-reported outcome (PRO) monitoring. We assessed understanding of longitudinal quality of life (QOL) profiles, presented as bar charts objectively and with self-ratings. In addition, patients' opinions on congruency of the QOL scores with their self-perceived health status were evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We recruited 40 brain tumour patients (57.5% female; mean age 52.7, SD 13.7). In total, 90% of patients rated the graphs as easy to understand. Accordingly, almost all questions on assessing understanding objectively were answered correctly by at least 80% of the patients. More than 95% indicated that the displayed QOL scores matched their personal perception of symptom burden and functional health in the observed period.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Patients are able to understand their QOL results when presented graphically and are able to interpret important changes. Displayed QOL scores obtained with the EORTC QLQ-C30 are consistent with the patients' personal perception of physical and emotional functioning, pain and fatigue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Knowledge about patients' understanding of graphically displayed QOL results contributes to creation of optimal evidence-based feedback on the patients' present QOL and its trajectory.</AbstractText>
